    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Muhammed Akhtar Zaman is a Swedish international technology entrepreneur known professionally as M. A. Zaman. He held senior Asia-Pacific responsibilities at Millicom and Kinnevik and became founding chairman and a shareholder of Cambodia’s mobile operator CamGSM. He later founded 6G AI Sweden, developing infrastructure for artificial-intelligence computing and cloud services.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "A meeting on an international flight helped M. A. Zaman assemble the design team for a new business. Sitting beside Porsche’s Asia-Pacific chief led to contact with Porsche Design Studio. Through Royal Falcon Fleet, Zaman brought the studio together with Australian naval architects Incat Crowther and interior specialists Greenline.\n\nHis commercial proposition was a series of fast catamarans offered through fractional ownership, borrowing a model used in private aviation. He saw potential customers in Asia and the Middle East. The resulting collaboration combined Austrian design, Australian engineering and Swedish construction, with the team coordinating across countries to turn the concept into a marketable product.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 89,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Full trade interview documents the business concept and team formation. Proposed commercial scale is not presented as achieved."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S3"
    ],
    "word_count": 104,
    "html": "<p>A meeting on an international flight helped M. A. Zaman assemble the design team for a new business. Sitting beside Porsche’s Asia-Pacific chief led to contact with Porsche Design Studio. Through Royal Falcon Fleet, Zaman brought the studio together with Australian naval architects Incat Crowther and interior specialists Greenline.</p>\r\n<p>His commercial proposition was a series of fast catamarans offered through fractional ownership, borrowing a model used in private aviation. He saw potential customers in Asia and the Middle East. The resulting collaboration combined Austrian design, Australian engineering and Swedish construction, with the team coordinating across countries to turn the concept into a marketable product.</p>\r\n"
  },
